## Outcome and conditions

Compare supplied concentration/temperature records using actual series, averaging, reference periods and uncertainty, without relabeling historical observations as current data. All essential components listed in metadata are required. Hypothetical assessment values instantiate the checked equations; they are not historical observations or observed outcomes. Independent checks are closed-note and without hints, solution access, external retrieval or other assistance; a basic arithmetic calculator is allowed. Teach establishes and records actual help/exposure conditions before claiming independence.

## Prerequisites and checks

- [O016 outcome and repair route](../lessons/O016.md#outcome-and-conditions): energy and climate mechanisms must be distinguished from any one sampled weather/temperature record.

O017-P1, item_version 1, prerequisite_diagnostic. **Prompt:** What does positive net energy flux imply for stored heat, and is a local one-day field the same object as a whole-system long-period heat inventory?

**Complete key and rubric:** Positive net flux accumulates heat; a location/day field and a multicomponent long-period inventory have different spatial/time/variable definitions. Require both; repair O016 external balance and heat inventory. Pass requires every requested reason and result; partial for an omitted reason or unresolved isolated arithmetic slip; fail for the conflicting mechanism. This diagnostic never counts as acquisition. Current or explicitly compatible committed independent prerequisite evidence may permit skipping its check; familiarity alone cannot. Use the cited complete prerequisite lesson or the named included foundation, then the worked example, after a partial/failing entry check.

## Teaching order and rationale

1. Use the diagnostic once to establish the necessary conceptual or algebraic foundation.
2. Present the complete checked-note explanation below, preserving variables, dates, assumptions and source qualifications. Follow one reasoning chain at a time so every intermediate step is visible.
3. Work W1 fully, asking the learner to explain why each step follows.
4. Use S1 as completion practice: show the setup and first identity, hide the final algebra/numbers and interpretation. After an explanation of the missing steps, withdraw support.
5. Present unused I1/I2 full checks with hidden solutions; use correction/repair after scoring the original answer. An unused D form can replace an exposed I only through a listed route.
6. On a later eligible occasion, begin an unused D item before recap. A numeric variant establishes bounded same-method performance, not general transfer.

## Motivation, intuition, and definitions

Compare supplied concentration/temperature records using actual series, averaging, reference periods and uncertainty, without relabeling historical observations as current data. Establish the definitions, assumptions and relevant comparisons before simplifying an equation. The complete reused passages establish every academic claim, notation choice and qualification assessed here; necessary prerequisites use the complete lessons above.

## Explanation steps

Origin: reused complete checked passage [[01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Economics_of_Green_Deal/Week_3/Greenhouse_Effect_CO2_and_Earth_Energy.md#Concentration is not a global daily average by default]]; fingerprint `0e546eb44a26562a6831079998cde23dd5004f51769540e9057950e69279b643`. Heading depth alone is adapted.

### Concentration is not a global daily average by default

A concentration in **ppm** measures a fraction of atmospheric molecules; Mauna Loa is a particular station, a daily/monthly average is not an annual average, and a global marine-surface estimate is not a station reading. The seasonal cycle on the Mauna Loa graphs reflects Northern Hemisphere vegetation uptake/decay over the year, superimposed on a long-term human-driven rise. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Economics_of_Green_Deal/Week_3/02_AQImp-ClimateChange.pdf#page=27|02_AQImp-ClimateChange.pdf, physical PDF p. 27–29]]

The source reports global 419.3 ppm in 2023, global 422.7 ppm in 2024, and a Mauna Loa daily 425.68 ppm on 1 November 2025; the last is a source-reported dated station value, not independently reconstructed daily data. Its 2024 “increase over 2023” 3.75 ppm is not the arithmetic difference of the displayed annual means (422.7−419.3=3.4). These statements mix vintages and annual-average versus within-year growth conventions. **Reference check:** NOAA's archived 21 May 2025 article reports global 2024 annual 422.8 ppm, within-2024 increase 3.75 ppm and Mauna Loa annual 424.61 ppm. This explains why year/series/averaging definition must accompany a number; the source 422.7 differs from that reference and from its own p. 30 figure 422.8. [NOAA, “Carbon dioxide levels in 2024”](https://content-drupal.climate.gov/news-features/understanding-climate/climate-change-atmospheric-carbon-dioxide). These are documented historical comparisons, not live 2026 concentrations. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Economics_of_Green_Deal/Week_3/02_AQImp-ClimateChange.pdf#page=28|02_AQImp-ClimateChange.pdf, physical PDF p. 28–30]]

Physical p. 30 compares an 800,000-year ice-core record (below about 300 ppm in that displayed record) with a modern dot. Its graphic labels **2024 average 422.8 ppm**, while the caption still says 2022. The observed lesson is the large, rapid recent rise relative to those glacial/interglacial variations; the inconsistent caption year is retained explicitly rather than relabeling the figure silently. The source attributes the underlying reconstruction to Lüthi et al. 2008/NOAA NCEI. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Economics_of_Green_Deal/Week_3/02_AQImp-ClimateChange.pdf#page=30|02_AQImp-ClimateChange.pdf, physical PDF p. 30]]


