### Reading the temperature evidence

Physical p. 32 is a deep-time reconstructed-temperature composite with **nonuniform time scales**: millions, then thousands of years, with geological periods and human-evolution labels. It illustrates long-run climate variation; straight visual slopes across scale changes do not measure comparable rates. Physical p. 33's heading mentions a “hockey stick dispute,” but its actual graph compares several historical temperature series over roughly 1840–2000. It is not, by itself, a millennium reconstruction or a resolution of every attribution dispute. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Economics_of_Green_Deal/Week_3/02_AQImp-ClimateChange.pdf#page=32|02_AQImp-ClimateChange.pdf, physical PDF p. 32–33]]

Physical p. 34 is explicitly a **one-day average 2 m temperature field for 25 February 2018** from GFS/ClimateReanalyzer. It shows spatial weather variation, not a global multi-decade trend or an anomaly with a stated climatology. Physical p. 35 instead gives a global land/sea temperature **anomaly relative to 1961–1990**, with an 1850–2023 plotted record and grey 95% confidence bounds. An anomaly is a difference from that reference mean, not the absolute surface temperature. It cannot be directly equated with a warming target relative to a different preindustrial baseline. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Economics_of_Green_Deal/Week_3/02_AQImp-ClimateChange.pdf#page=34|02_AQImp-ClimateChange.pdf, physical PDF p. 34–35]]


## Worked example

O017-W1, item_version 1, worked_example. **Prompt:** A synthetic dataset labels A as global annual CO2 $410\ \mathrm{ppm}$ in year1, B as global annual CO2 $414\ \mathrm{ppm}$ in year2, and C as one station’s one-day CO2 $418\ \mathrm{ppm}$ in year2. Compute the annual-mean difference and explain why C−B is not automatically global annual growth. A temperature record reports anomaly $1.2^\circ\mathrm{C}$ relative to 1961–1990, while another figure is a one-day absolute2m temperature map. Explain what each can show and why no direct comparison to a different-reference climate target follows. Diagnose the source’s 2024 annual means / within-year increase and 2022 caption / 2024 dot differences, the source-reported November 2025 daily value, nonuniform deep-time axes and scope of its historical-series “hockey-stick” heading.

**Complete solution and essential rubric:** The synthetic common-series annual-mean change is $414-410=4.000000\ \mathrm{ppm}$. A particular station/day has another location/averaging definition, so its gap from the global annual mean is not that growth measure. Seasonal cycles can coexist with a long-term rise. The $1.2^\circ\mathrm{C}$ anomaly is a difference from the stated 1961–1990 reference mean, not absolute temperature; a one-day 2 m map is weather/spatial variation, not multi-decade climate change. A different-baseline target needs a justified baseline comparison, not an unqualified equality. Source annual means give $422.7-419.3=3.4$ ppm; the cited 3.75 ppm is within-2024 growth, while archived NOAA annual values differ slightly (global 422.8, Mauna Loa 424.61). Preserve the source’s 2024 graphic dot versus 2022 caption conflict and label 425.68 ppm on 1 November 2025 as source-reported station/day data not independently reconstructed. These are historical statements, not current 2026 observations. Deep-time panels have nonuniform time scales, so visual slopes across them are not comparable rates. The source heading’s actual plotted historical series are about 1840–2000, not by themselves a millennium reconstruction or a resolution of all attribution questions. Sample graphics / 95% bounds must retain record, baseline, coverage and uncertainty. All requested calculations, derivations and qualifications are essential; the component map above applies to every full form.

